WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.435
TLS security has to be maintained over time.

2
00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:15.203
Tests should confirm that production-like clients validate certificates,
reject unexpected names, handle expired or untrusted certificates safely
at a high level, and do not inherit development-only verification
bypasses.

3
00:00:15.203 --> 00:00:24.219
Server deployment tests should confirm that HTTPS assumptions, proxy
behavior, secure cookies, and backend trust boundaries match the intended
architecture.

4
00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:35.934
Dependency and library review matters because TLS behavior is affected by
HTTP clients, database drivers, message clients, runtimes, operating
system trust stores, container images, and platform defaults.

5
00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:50.149
Guidance on protocol versions and cipher choices changes over time, so
durable engineering practice focuses on using maintained libraries,
current platform defaults, clear configuration ownership, and timely
updates rather than hardcoded folklore.

6
00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:55.414
Logging and monitoring should make TLS failures visible without leaking
sensitive material.

7
00:00:55.414 --> 00:01:03.641
Logs can include safe error categories, service names, certificate
expiration warnings, connection error trends, and correlation identifiers.

8
00:01:03.641 --> 00:01:10.288
They should avoid private keys, tokens, credentials, full sensitive
payloads, or unnecessary certificate material.

9
00:01:10.288 --> 00:01:18.383
Monitoring should watch certificate expiration, renewal failures,
handshake errors, configuration drift, and unusual connection failures.

10
00:01:18.383 --> 00:01:26.610
Incident response planning should include private key exposure, misissued
certificates, unexpected trust-store changes, and failed renewals.

11
00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:34.968
Teams should know who owns the certificate, where the private key lives,
which services use it, how to rotate or revoke it, and how to validate
recovery.

12
00:01:34.968 --> 00:01:37.995
TLS lifecycle management is not a one-time setup task.

13
00:01:37.995 --> 00:01:44.840
It is an ongoing operational responsibility that follows services,
domains, dependencies, and infrastructure changes.
